<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">José de Alencar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1829–1877</span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">José de Alencar was a Brazilian novelist, playwright and politician, a leading figure of Brazilian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/romanticism">Romanticism</a>. His novels O Guarani (1857) and Iracema (1865) idealised the Indigenous peoples and the founding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brazil">Brazil</a>, helping to create a national literature distinct from that of Portugal. He also served as a Conservative deputy and as minister of justice under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pedro-ii-of-brazil">Pedro II</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jose-de-alencar.jpg" alt="José de Alencar" width="320" height="432" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Alberto Henschel · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jose_de_Alencar.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
